-- Adds the UNIQUE index used by the optional demo-data loader.
--
-- This migration used to insert 50 hand-picked rows (Google DNS,
-- Cloudflare, RFC 1918, …) so that /lookup would answer something
-- before the first ETL run. That was wrong for a production
-- deployment: a fresh database ended up with 50 demo rows that LOOK
-- like real coverage. An operator who skipped the seed step still got
-- them, because the seed was part of `migrate`, not part of an
-- explicit choice. A /lookup hit on 8.8.8.8 said "Mountain View" while
-- every other address came back "not found" — a database that reads
-- as populated but is not.
--
-- The demo rows are now opt-in:
--   - production: nothing. Run `node dist/cli.js update` to load the
--     real DB-IP library.
--   - development: `node dist/cli.js seed:demo` loads them explicitly.
--
-- The 50 rows themselves live in src/storage/seed/demo-ranges.sql and
-- are applied by that command, not by the migration runner. Migration
-- 025 removes the rows that earlier versions of this migration
-- inserted, so an upgraded database ends up in the same state as a
-- fresh one.
--
-- The index stays. The demo loader inserts with INSERT IGNORE and
-- needs row-level idempotency so re-running it is harmless. It is
-- UNIQUE on (start_ip, end_ip, country_source) rather than on
-- (start_ip, end_ip) because ETL rows use other source values such as
-- 'dbip' — so the index does not constrain them, and migration 013's
-- separate uniq_start_end key remains the one the ETL's upsert path
-- relies on.

ALTER TABLE ip_ranges ADD UNIQUE INDEX uniq_seed (start_ip, end_ip, country_source);
